  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains in control Friday into the weekend with\ndrier conditions and warming temperatures to about 5 degrees\nabove normal. Hurricane Rachel looms just off the Baja and it\nappears like the high pressure will remain in position to keep\nthis moisture from advecting northward. With the high \npositioned over the Great Basin and Hurricane Rachel off the \nBaja, it almost forms a Rex Block. Some high level moisture will\nmove into the area from a trough moving towards the west coast,\nresulting in some high cirrus across the region this weekend. \nModels are indicating some isolated storms potentially forming \nover the San Juans Sunday afternoon. This eastern Pacific trough\nopens up and moves over top of the ridge on Tuesday as an open \nshortwave with some isolated to scattered storms possible over \nthe high terrain. Aside from that, conditions will remain mostly\ndry through the coming week. The high will build back to the \nwest which further steers Hurricane Rachel westward out to sea. \nHigh pressure ridge will shift eastward and remain overhead for \nthe remainder of the week with a Pacific Northwest trough \nhelping steer remnants of this tropical system up and around the\nhigh and keeping us warm and dry through the remainder of the \nweek. High temperatures remain 5 to 10 degrees above normal \nthrough the first week of October.",
